Figure 4 shows plots of the equalizer output radii as a
function of time for RDE and CMA blind equalization                             LT
algorithms using the same blind carrier recovery scheme.
Notice that the four radii start to become more distinct after             cn"
about 8 seconds when using RDE. When using CMA, the
equalizer converges after about 9-10 seconds but the radii               2-
are not as distinct as they are for RDE. This is because CMA
is not an optimal criterion for 16 QAM. The blind carrier                 n
acquisition was also started at time = 0. The system cannot
be switched into decision directed mode until after the carrier          a-
has locked. Figure 5 shows the carrier offset frequency as a
function of time. Before the equalizer converges, the carrier                  LT
hovers about its initial value. Once the equalizer has
converged, the carrier recovery acquires very quickly when       Figure 4. Equalizer output modulus versus time when using
using RDE. The carrier is also acquired when using CMA but       the RDE and CMA algorithms for blind equalization.

not as quickly. Carrier lock is determined by measuring the
phase error power. Figure 6 shows the phase error power for
carrier recovery loop using both RDE and CMA. For RDE, the
error power drops from about 22.5 degrees to about 9
degrees. For CMA, differentiation between unlock and lock is
much less distinct, only about a degree. This is again due to
the fact that the RDE is an optimal criterion for QAM signals
and CMA, although it works, is not. Figure 7 shows the
equalizer responses for both RDE and CMA. The RDE
equalizer response did not change once the equalizer was
switched into decision directed mode. For CMA, however, the
equalizer response converges to the same shape as the RDE
equalizer once switched into decision directed mode.

